      marks: 9, command: 'Compare',
      modelAnswer: 'Regular languages are the simplest class in the Chomsky hierarchy and are exactly those that can be specified by a regular expression or recognised by a finite state machine. They suit patterns where the recogniser needs only constant memory regardless of input length — for example, "any string ending in .txt" or "any sequence of digits". They cannot describe languages that require counting unboundedly: classic examples that are not regular include {aⁿbⁿ | n ≥ 0} (n a\'s followed by exactly n b\'s) and balanced parentheses, because the recogniser would have to remember how many a\'s it had seen, which is not possible with a finite number of states. Context-free languages are a strictly larger class, specified by context-free grammars (such as those written in BNF) and recognised by pushdown automata — finite state machines equipped with an unbounded stack. The stack supplies the unbounded memory needed for matched-pair structures. Context-free languages can describe balanced parentheses, arithmetic expressions and most of the syntax of common programming languages such as Pascal, C and Java; that is why BNF is the workhorse of compiler design. However, even context-free grammars cannot describe certain features such as identifier reuse (declared-before-used) or context-sensitive type rules, which compilers handle in a separate semantic-analysis pass. Practically: regular languages → token recognition (lexing); context-free languages → syntax (parsing). Both classes are formally decidable — questions like membership can be answered algorithmically in polynomial time — which is one reason they are used as the formal foundation of compiler tooling.'
